Yes 👍 This is already a good README/project documentation structure, but I would improve it so that in the future you can quickly understand what each EC2 deployment is, how it works, where the code is, and how to restart/debug it.

I recommend keeping your README like this:

Django AWS Deployment Lab

This EC2 instance contains three different Django deployment approaches that I built and tested for learning and real-world deployment practice.

Production/Test URL:
http://3.110.165.35/stu/

AWS Infrastructure
EC2

EC2 is used as the application server and hosts the Django deployments.

EC2 Public IP:

3.110.165.35
Database

PostgreSQL is hosted on:

AWS RDS PostgreSQL

The Django applications connect to RDS through the private network/security group configuration.

Django Application
       │
       │ PostgreSQL : 5432
       ▼
AWS RDS PostgreSQL

The RDS Security Group allows PostgreSQL access from the EC2 Security Group.

File Storage

Django media files are stored in:

AWS S3

Architecture:

Django
   │
   │ Upload
   ▼
AWS S3
   │
   │ Image/File URL
   ▼
Browser

For production, the EC2 instance uses an IAM Role to access S3 instead of storing AWS access keys directly on the EC2 server.

EC2 SSH Access

Set the correct permission on the PEM key:

chmod 400 yourpemkey.pem

Connect to EC2:

ssh -i yourpemkey.pem ubuntu@YOUR_EC2_IP

Example:

ssh -i yourpemkey.pem ubuntu@3.110.165.35
Deployment 1 — Direct Git Clone Deployment
Project
django_blogs_pro

This deployment uses the traditional approach where the Django source code is directly cloned from GitHub onto the EC2 instance.

Architecture:

GitHub Repository
       │
       │ git clone / git pull
       ▼
EC2
       │
       ▼
Django Source Code
       │
       ▼
Gunicorn
       │
       ▼
Nginx
       │
       ├──────────► AWS RDS PostgreSQL
       │
       └──────────► AWS S3

Typical workflow:

git clone YOUR_REPOSITORY
cd django_blogs_pro

Update application:

git pull origin main

Restart application after changes if using a process manager:

sudo systemctl restart gunicorn
sudo systemctl restart nginx
Advantages
Simple to understand
Easy for learning
Direct access to source code on EC2
Easy to debug files directly
Disadvantages
Manual deployment
Server depends on the source code being present
Environment differences can cause problems
Requires manual dependency installation/build steps
Not ideal for scalable production deployment
Deployment 2 — Docker Image Deployment
Project
django_production

This deployment uses a Docker image stored in Docker Hub.

Architecture:

Local Development
       │
       │ Docker Build
       ▼
Docker Image
       │
       │ Docker Push
       ▼
Docker Hub
       │
       │ Docker Pull
       ▼
EC2
       │
       ▼
Docker Container
       │
       ├──────────► AWS RDS PostgreSQL
       │
       └──────────► AWS S3

The Django source code is packaged inside the Docker image.

EC2 does not need to run:

git pull

for Django application code updates.

Instead:

docker pull IMAGE_NAME
docker compose up -d

or:

docker compose pull
docker compose up -d
Advantages
Consistent environment
Dependencies are packaged into the image
Easier deployment
EC2 does not need the full source code
Easier to move the application to another server
Disadvantages
Docker knowledge required
Image build and registry management required
Need to manage image versions
Must consider CPU architecture
Deployment 3 — CI/CD Deployment
Project
django_cicd

This is the most complete deployment approach.

The deployment process is automated using GitHub Actions and Docker.

Architecture:

Developer
    │
    │ git push
    ▼
GitHub Repository
    │
    ▼
GitHub Actions
    │
    ├── Run Tests
    │
    ├── Build Docker Image
    │
    ├── Build Correct Platform
    │
    ├── Push Image
    │
    ▼
Docker Hub
    │
    │ Pull
    ▼
EC2
    │
    ▼
Docker Compose
    │
    ├──────────► Django
    │
    ├──────────► Nginx
    │
    └──────────► Redis
                │
                ├──► AWS RDS PostgreSQL
                │
                └──► AWS S3

The goal is:

Developer
    │
    │ git push
    ▼
GitHub
    │
    ▼
GitHub Actions
    │
    ▼
Docker Hub
    │
    ▼
EC2 automatically updated

The developer should not need to manually SSH into EC2 for every deployment.

CI/CD Secrets

Sensitive values should be stored in:

GitHub Actions Secrets

Examples:

DOCKER_USERNAME
DOCKER_PASSWORD / DOCKER_TOKEN
EC2_HOST
EC2_USERNAME
EC2_SSH_KEY

Never hard-code these values inside the repository.

Comparison of Three Deployment Methods
Deployment	Code Location	Build	Deployment	Best Use
Direct Git Clone	EC2	On EC2	Manual	Learning / Simple Apps
Docker Image	Docker Image	Local/CI	Manual Pull	Container Deployment
Docker + CI/CD	Docker Image	GitHub Actions	Automatic	Production Workflow
Current AWS Architecture
                    Internet
                       │
                       ▼
                  EC2 Instance
                       │
                 Nginx / Docker
                       │
                       ▼
                    Django
                  /         \
                 /           \
                ▼             ▼
        AWS RDS PostgreSQL   AWS S3
                │             │
                │             │
                └──────┬──────┘
                       │
                     Redis

