Redis
Important Production Configuration
Environment Variables

Never commit .env to GitHub.

Add:

.env

to .gitignore.

Production secrets should be stored securely.

Current examples include:

SECRET_KEY
DB_PASSWORD
AWS credentials (if ever required)

For EC2 → S3 access, prefer:

EC2
  ↓
IAM Role
  ↓
S3

instead of hard-coding AWS access keys on EC2.

Database Architecture

Local development:

Django
   ↓
Docker PostgreSQL

Production:

Django
   ↓
AWS RDS PostgreSQL

Production should not depend on a PostgreSQL container running on the EC2 instance when RDS is being used.

Run migrations after deployment:

docker compose exec web python manage.py migrate
Static and Media Files
Static Files

Static files:

CSS
JavaScript
Django Admin Static

are collected using:

docker compose exec web python manage.py collectstatic --noinput

Nginx serves static files.

Architecture:

Browser
   │
   ▼
Nginx
   │
   ▼
Static Volume
Media Files

User-uploaded files and images are stored in:

AWS S3

Architecture:

Django
   │
   │ Upload
   ▼
AWS S3
Docker Commands Reference

Check running containers:

docker ps

Check all containers:

docker ps -a

Check images:

docker images

Check Docker Compose services:

docker compose ps

Start:

docker compose up -d

Build:

docker compose build

Build and start:

docker compose up -d --build

Stop:

docker compose down

Pull latest images:

docker compose pull

View logs:

docker compose logs

View web logs:

docker compose logs web

Follow logs:

docker compose logs -f web

View last 100 lines:

docker compose logs --tail=100 web

Enter Django container:

docker compose exec web bash

Run Django command:

docker compose exec web python manage.py migrate

Create superuser:

docker compose exec web python manage.py createsuperuser

Collect static files:

docker compose exec web python manage.py collectstatic --noinput

Check container environment:

docker compose exec web env
Docker Debugging Workflow

When the application does not work:

1. Check containers
docker compose ps
2. Check logs
docker compose logs --tail=100 web
3. Check Nginx logs
docker compose logs --tail=100 nginx
4. Check Docker containers
docker ps
5. Check application inside container
docker compose exec web python manage.py check
6. Check database connection
docker compose exec web python manage.py migrate
7. Check static files
docker compose exec web ls -la /app/staticfiles
8. Check Nginx can access static files
docker compose exec nginx ls -la /app/staticfiles
9. Test static URL
curl -I http://localhost/static/css/main.css
10. Check ports
sudo ss -tulpn
Important Lessons Learned
Docker Image Architecture

The development machine may use:

ARM64

while EC2 may use:

AMD64 / x86_64

Check EC2:

uname -m

For an AMD64 EC2 deployment from an Apple Silicon Mac:

docker build --platform linux/amd64 -t IMAGE_NAME .

For production CI/CD, the preferred solution is to let GitHub Actions build the correct production image.

Future Production Improvements

The next improvements should be:

1. HTTPS

Add:

Domain
   ↓
HTTPS / SSL
   ↓
Nginx

Use a valid domain and SSL certificate.

2. CI/CD

Automate:

git push
    ↓
GitHub Actions
    ↓
Tests
    ↓
Docker Build
    ↓
Docker Hub
    ↓
EC2 Deployment
3. Monitoring

Learn and configure:

AWS CloudWatch

Monitor:

EC2 CPU
Memory
Disk
Application logs
Nginx logs
Errors
4. Alerts

Configure:

CloudWatch
    ↓
CloudWatch Alarm
    ↓
SNS
    ↓
Email Alert
5. Database Backups

Configure RDS:

Automated backups
Point-in-time recovery
Backup retention
Multi-AZ for higher availability
6. S3 Protection

Configure:

Block Public Access
IAM least privilege
Versioning
Encryption
Lifecycle policies
7. Load Balancing

For scaling:

Internet
   ↓
Application Load Balancer
   │
   ├── EC2 Instance 1
   │
   └── EC2 Instance 2
8. Container Orchestration

After becoming comfortable with Docker + CI/CD:

EC2
  ↓
ECS
  ↓
EKS / Kubernetes
Deployment Philosophy

The three deployments represent the evolution of a real deployment process:

Level 1
Direct Git Clone
       ↓
Understand Linux + EC2 + Nginx + Gunicorn

Level 2
Docker Image
       ↓
Understand Containers + Docker + Docker Compose

Level 3
Docker + CI/CD
       ↓
Understand Automation + Continuous Deployment

Next
       ↓
HTTPS + Monitoring + Scaling
       ↓
ECS
       ↓
Kubernetes / EKS

The current setup is a strong learning foundation for a Python/Django backend developer moving toward production and cloud deployment.

My main advice

Your documentation is good, but definitely add the "Current AWS Architecture", "Three Deployment Comparison", "Debugging Workflow", and "Future Production Improvements" sections. These will be the most useful when you revisit this project months later.

Also, I recommend keeping three separate deployment directories as you currently do, because they demonstrate three distinct approaches:

django_blogs_pro
    → Direct source-code deployment

django_production
    → Docker image deployment

django_cicd
    → Docker + GitHub Actions CI/CD

One thing I would do next is finish django_cicd completely. Your ideal final pipeline should be:

git push
    ↓
GitHub Actions
    ↓
Run tests
    ↓
Build AMD64 Docker image
    ↓
Push to Docker Hub
    ↓
SSH to EC2
    ↓
docker compose pull
    ↓
docker compose up -d
    ↓
Health check
    ↓
Deployment successful 🚀

After that, add HTTPS + CloudWatch monitoring + automated backups. That will give you a very solid end-to-end production deployment project to discuss in interviews.